<p>Understanding risk in context often requires numbers to be "adjusted": for example, when investigating the link between smoking and lung cancer, researchers adjust the number of observed cases of lung cancer that are attributed to smoking to account for factors like age, sex, lifestyle, or air pollution. This ensures that any comparison between study groups is fair, but means results can look very different to the raw data to a lay reader.</p>



<p>If you are having trouble understanding risks, see if you can find graphics which visualise it. When you are communicating risks, consider using graphics. </p>



                    
